\begin{proof}
Suppose that $A\subseteq X$ is non-empty, open, and closed. Since $A$ is non-empty, $X\backslash A\neq X$. Because $X\backslash A$ is open in the finite complement topology, it must therefore be finite. If $A\neq X$, then $A$ is also a nonempty open proper subset, so $A$ is finite. This would make $X=A\cup(X\backslash A)$ finite, contradicting that $X$ is infinite. Hence $A=X$, and therefore $X$ is connected.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $A$ be a non-empty clopen subset of $X$. If $A\neq X$, then both $A$ and $X\backslash A$ are proper open subsets of $X$. In the finite complement topology, every proper open subset is finite. Thus $X=A\cup(X\backslash A)$ is finite, a contradiction. Consequently, every non-empty clopen subset is $X$ itself. Since $\varnothing$ and $X$ are the only clopen subsets, $X$ is connected.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Assume, toward a contradiction, that $X$ is disconnected. Then there exists a non-empty proper clopen subset $A\subsetneq X$. Since $A$ is open and proper in the finite complement topology, $A$ is finite. Since $X\backslash A$ is also open and proper, it is finite as well. Therefore $X=A\cup(X\backslash A)$ is finite, contradicting the assumption that $X$ is infinite. Hence $X$ is connected.
\end{proof}

